  "worked_solution": "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)-\\frac{2}{5}(x-3)^{2}+\u22ef+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}n\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{4}{5}(x-3)+\u22ef+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}n\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}.\n$$",
